Heidelberg in southwest Germany is a  famous tourist destination it has a  beautiful old town and a ruined castle  on the hill but it also has a modern  side a three thousand unit residential  complex built to passive house standards  is going up on the site of an old goods  yard pet repair scene and her family  were among the first to move in two  years ago people and appliances give off  so much heat but hardly any additional  heating is needed in a badly built house  or if you have single glazed windows or  patio door they'll always be a draft  around your feet or you feel it in your  neck and back and the floor will be cold  your feet feel the cold first that just  doesn't happen in a passive house once  you've got used to it you can't imagine  living anywhere that's poorly insulated  energy expert Hobart pash was involved  in the design  he says closed windows and good  insulation are key if you knock on the  wall you hear that there's no masonry  just behind the surface but a lot of  insulation about 30 centimeters thick  usually made of styrofoam then it's  coated with insulating stucco  the windows and patio doors have triple  glazing and there's a good ventilation  system that keeps the air circulating  doesn't pacify his eye nowadays it's  easy to build homes to passive house  standards off the rack so to speak  non-residential buildings are harder  there you have to take a closer look at  the use and come up with a tailor-made  solution it's more of a challenge for  everybody involved for example the local  kindergarten and an office building  still under construction sometimes  they'll be empty other times full the  ventilation heating and cooling system  has to cope with the fluctuations  further south in the Harvin's book  stands the world's first museum built to  these exacting standards you can feel  the air is really good here the  atmosphere is great people like to spend  time here the temperature doesn't vary  wildly as it would with a conventional  air-conditioning system  art lovers feel good here and curators  are fascinated by the innovative  technology I had my doubts because there  were no comparable buildings but in fact  it fulfills all the conditions we set to  keep our works of art in optimal  condition about 20,000 people a year  visit the museum they represent a  challenge for the climate control system  visitors are problematic because they  not only generate heat they also bring  in pollutants from outside and moisture  which isn't great for paintings so the  ventilation had to be up to dealing with  those challenges sensor send data to the  control unit which responds to the  changing conditions behind the scenes it  takes a lot of equipment to make it all  work passive house principles can be  deployed all over the world there's an  independent Research Institute in  Darmstadt devoted to developing the  passive house concept the implementation  of the principles has to match the  climatic conditions of each specific  location Africa is generally warmer than  Germany so you tend to need less thermal  insulation there and you can use double  rather than triple glazing  you can often meet passive house  standards thereby simpler means than in  Germany at the housing complex in  Heidelberg heating costs tend to be just  one tenth of what they would be in  normal homes and Pitka Beijing for one  says there's hardly any downside if I'm  cooking or I have guests there might be  eight of us sitting here at the table  we'll be eating or playing a game and  then sometimes it does get hot eight of  us are letting off heat there are two  options either you open the window or  you turn the ventilation system up  higher that speeds up the flow of air so  the warm air is distributed much faster  through the house one thing you have to  get used to is not to open the windows  all the time and in summer it's  important to keep sunlight off the  windows so the interior doesn't heat up  too much it's easier to 